    "lawCode": "Amd §483, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8067\n\nSPONSOR: RITCHIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the real property tax law, in relation to the real prop-\nerty tax exemption for structures and buildings essential to agricul-\ntural and horticultural use\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nWould extend a property tax exemption for buildings essential to farm-\ning.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThe bill would amend subdivision one of Section 483 of the Real Property\nTax Law to extend provisions granting a one-time, ten year property tax\nexemption for any increase in property value from the construction or\nimprovement of structures that are essential to the operation of the\nfarm and are actually used for that purpose. The law will be extended\nuntil January 1st, 2029.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nOur farmers toil day in and day out to produce the dairy products, vege-\ntables, fruits, beverages and other products that we all enjoy.  They do\nthis through good market conditions and bad, tremendously productive\nweather seasons and conditions where their entire output is lost to\nweather or disease. In addition, our farmers are competing in the\nmarketplace with other states and countries where the costs of producing\nthe same products are much lower than in New York.  Infrastructure costs\nfor farm buildings can be extremely expensive for these family owned\nbusinesses that some years don't see a profit or experience losses.\n\nTo assist our farmers, section 483 of the real property tax law has, for\nalmost fifty years, exempted from real property taxation improvements to\nthose buildings essential to the production of agricultural goods.\nBecause of this long standing tax break, local governments will not\nexperience a sudden drop in assessed value. This ten year property tax\nexemption has proved to be extremely helpful to our farmers who rely on\nit to lower their cost of production so that they can stay in business,\nmaintain open space especially in highly developed areas, and continue\nto feed their fellow New Yorkers.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
